;
TROUBLESHOOTING AND NETWORK OPERATION

A - When something goes wrong
B - Inviting callers from elsewhere

:A
WHEN SOMETHING GOES WRONG

Symptom - First things to examine
  Launcher won't start - Host permissions, occupied port, another running copy
  Caller connects but sees nothing - Raw TCP rather than Telnet; press RETURN
  DIR or LT works locally but not remotely - A0: caller read-only permission
  and F4
  Game won't prepare its files - MOUNT GAMES; I0: RW; approval of the actual
  game
  Old names after MOUNT - NDSCAN errors, marker collisions
  AUTH refuses to run - Private operation with BYE attached
  AUTH settings vanish after restart - Saved I13:AUTH.DAT
  HELP or board records missing - Correct I: image and DU permissions
  Garbled CHAT - ANSI/VT100 setup, or CHAT /S
  WORK files vanish - Expected at call boundaries

Try to decide whether the trouble lies in the host, CP/M, BYE or a transient
program. If a command works in private CP/M but not for a caller, look at
permissions before rebuilding software. The AUTH appendix goes into more
detail about recovering a bad policy.

:B
INVITING CALLERS FROM ELSEWHERE

Once you are happy with local calls, stop the system and change ADDRESS to the
appropriate host IPv4 address, or 0.0.0.0 for all interfaces. Restart. The
host firewall and router must permit the caller port. For Docker, arrange the
host port publication instead; see the Docker guide.

Make a call from another computer before announcing the board. Raw TCP
provides no encryption; advise callers not to reuse important passwords. Keep
administrative tools and credentials private, approve only reviewed software,
and retain a good stopped backup. Guest permission checks are not a substitute
for securing the host itself.

Automatic startup, scheduled backups, monitoring and other unattended hosting
arrangements are separate matters. Optional administration guides are
available for Linux, macOS and Windows. None is needed for ordinary foreground
operation.
